      deepTheory: `<p>Professional hygiene is infection control, not tidiness. The chain of infection needs six links — infectious agent, reservoir, exit portal, transmission mode, entry portal, susceptible host — and your job is to break at least one link at every service. In salon work the dominant transmission modes are <b>direct contact</b> (hands, lesions), <b>indirect contact</b> (shears, combs, capes, towels) and <b>airborne droplets/dust</b> (bleach persulphates, fungal spores from feet-adjacent laundry, aerosolised product).</p><p><b>Hand hygiene (adapted WHO 5 moments):</b> before touching a client, before clean/aseptic steps (e.g. handling disinfected tools), after body-fluid/exposure risk (blood from a nick, pus), after touching a client, after touching surroundings. Alcohol rub (≥60% ethanol) for visibly clean hands, 20–30 seconds; soap and water 40–60 seconds when soiled. Short nails, no polish/chips that harbour microbes, no rings except a plain band.</p><p><b>Disinfection hierarchy you must be able to recite:</b> cleaning (detergent + water removes soil — always first, because organic matter inactivates most disinfectants) → disinfection (hospital/salon-grade quaternary ammonium or accelerated hydrogen peroxide, full label contact time, typically 3–10 minutes — a 10-second dunk is theatre) → sterilisation (autoclave for anything puncturing skin; not required for standard cutting tools but required for microneedling/extractors that draw blood). Barbicide-style concentrate is commonly 30 ml per litre (check your label — never guess); solution changed daily or when visibly soiled; tools fully immersed, then rinsed, dried and stored covered.</p><p><b>Laundry and sharps:</b> capes, towels and neck strips at 60°C minimum with detergent (thermal disinfection threshold); soiled linen in covered bins, never on the floor. Blood spill protocol — gloves, cover with paper towels, clean with detergent, disinfect, dispose in clinical waste bag, wash hands, record in the incident book. Single-use items (neck strips, spatulas, gloves, masks) are never re-used between clients.</p><p><b>Shear metallurgy and ergonomics:</b> entry shears are 420/440A stainless; professional Japanese convex shears are typically 440C, VG-10 or cobalt alloys (59–62 HRC) — harder steel holds a finer edge longer but chips if dropped, hence the covered dry storage rule. Pivot tension: blades should fall to ~45° under their own weight when held open (the drop test); too tight and you crush-cut and strain the thumb, too loose and hair folds. Repetitive strain is the industry's chronic injury — neutral wrist, elbow near 90°, shear weight under ~50 g for long sessions, alternate tasks, and stretch finger extensors daily.</p><p><b>Chemical awareness (WHMIS/GHS literacy):</b> read pictograms (corrosion, exclamation, flame), signal words (Danger vs Warning) and precautionary statements before opening anything new. Keep an inventory list, safety data sheets accessible, decanted bottles fully labelled (product + hazard + date), flammables away from heat sources and candles. Ventilation target: continuous fresh-air exchange in the mixing zone — open windows/door plus a fan moving air outward, not recirculating fumes across the salon.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference table — disinfection at a glance:</b> low-level (quats, 3–10 min contact) for non-critical tools like combs and shears after cleaning; intermediate (accelerated hydrogen peroxide, tuberculocidal claims) for blood-exposure incidents; high-level/sterilisation (autoclave 121°C/15 psi/15–30 min with indicators) for anything puncturing skin. Dirty solution, short contact, or skipping the detergent clean first each downgrade you a full level — the most common inspection failure in salons. Cross-check monthly: solution change log, expiry dates, covered storage, labelled decant bottles.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The nicked ear', brief: 'Midway through a one-length cut on a walk-in client, your shear tip nicks the ear. A bead of blood appears. The client is calm but watching you. Walk through the next 10 minutes to professional standard.', tasks: ['List your immediate actions in order (client, wound, tools, station).', 'Which waste stream do the bloodied tissue and neck strip go into, and what laundry temperature do they need?', 'What do you record, and who (if anyone) needs telling before the next client sits down?', 'What changes to your shear handling and positioning would have prevented this?'], debrief: 'Gloves on → compress with clean tissue → antiseptic if trained → adhesive strip → reassure and offer to pause. Bloodied disposables to clinical waste; reusables laundered at 60°C+. Record in the incident book and re-disinfect the shear (blood = full contact-time disinfection). Prevention: closed shears when repositioning, points down, cut with the client still, announce near ears.' },
