      deepTheory: `<p>Hair is 65–95% keratin protein by dry weight, and its behaviour in your hands is pure materials science. The <b>cuticle</b> (6–10 overlapping scale layers, like roof tiles pointing tip-ward) controls friction, shine and chemical entry; raised cuticle = dull, tangly, fast-absorbing hair. The <b>cortex</b> (spindle-shaped cortical cells packed with keratin macrofibrils) provides ~90% of tensile strength and houses melanin granules — eumelanin (brown/black, dense oval granules) and pheomelanin (red/yellow, diffuse). The <b>medulla</b> (fragmented air-filled core, often absent in fine hair) contributes little mechanically. Each follicle angles into the dermis with an attached arrector pili muscle, sebaceous gland and dermal papilla blood supply — the papilla is the living factory; the visible shaft is dead tissue, which is why conditioning coats rather than heals.</p><p><b>Bond chemistry that governs everything you do:</b> hydrogen bonds (break with water/heat — this is why blow-drying reshapes hair and humidity ruins it), salt bonds (pH-sensitive), and disulphide bonds (sulphur bridges — only broken by chemical reduction/oxidation: perms, relaxers, bleach). Wet hair stretches because water plasticises the keratin; healthy hair extends 30–50% and recovers, a direct readout of intact cortex.</p><p><b>Growth cycle in numbers:</b> anagen 2–7 years (~85–90% of scalp hair, growing ~1–1.5 cm/month), catagen 2–3 weeks (~1%), telogen ~3 months (~10–15%, then exogen shedding). Normal shedding 50–100 hairs/day; a sudden jump 2–3 months after illness, childbirth or crash dieting is classic telogen effluvium — recognise it and refer to a GP/dermatologist rather than selling treatments. Androgenetic patterns (receding hairline, crown thinning, widening part) need medical referral; cosmetic camouflage is your lane, diagnosis is not.</p><p><b>Classification systems professionals use:</b> Andre Walker chart (1 straight → 2 wavy → 3 curly → 4 coily, with a/b/c sublevels) and the FIA system (texture F/M/C + pattern + porosity) for curl, plus the salon quartet for chemistry decisions — texture (strand diameter: fine &lt;60 µm, medium, coarse &gt;80 µm), density (hairs/cm²: ponytail circumference is a practical proxy — &lt;5 cm thin, 5–10 medium, &gt;10 cm thick), porosity (float + slip + drying-time tests: low porosity beads water and resists colour; high porosity soaks and fades) and elasticity. Document all four on every chemical consultation card.</p><p><b>Scalp conditions to identify on sight:</b> pityriasis capitis (dry dandruff — small white non-inflammatory flakes), seborrhoeic dermatitis (oily yellowish adherent flakes with erythema — refer if severe), psoriasis (thick silvery plaques extending past the hairline — refer), pediculosis (nits cemented at an angle near the scalp — refuse service), tinea capitis (circular scaly patches, possible black-dot broken hairs — refuse + medical referral), traction alopecia (marginal thinning from tight styles — restyle loosely + refer if scarring). Rule of thumb: flakes without redness can be managed; redness, pustules, swelling, or anything spreading gets referred.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference table — testing matrix:</b> texture by roll-and-slip (fine slips silently, coarse rasps); density by ponytail circumference (&lt;5 cm thin, 5–10 medium, &gt;10 thick); porosity by float (floats = low, slow-sink = medium, instant-sink = high) confirmed by drying time (low &gt;4 hrs air-dry, high &lt;1.5 hrs); elasticity by wet stretch (&lt;20% brittle, 30–50% healthy, gummy = chemically compromised). Record all four plus scalp state on every card. Re-test quarterly — porosity and elasticity move with chemical history, and last season's virgin hair is this season's porous lengths.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The fading redhead', brief: 'A client with fine, high-porosity hair had permanent copper colour 3 weeks ago and it has already faded to dull blonde at the ends. She wants it re-done stronger with 30 vol "so it sticks". Analyse and prescribe.', tasks: ['Explain the real cause using porosity + cuticle + cortex science (why stronger developer is the wrong answer).', 'Which tests prove your diagnosis in-salon in under 5 minutes?', 'Write the corrected plan: product type, volume, porosity equaliser, and home-care.', 'What maintenance schedule and aftercare prevent the next fade?'], debrief: 'High porosity + raised cuticle = colour molecules wash out; 30 vol further raises cuticle and worsens fade. Prove with float/stretch tests. Correct plan: demi or permanent with 10–20 vol + porosity filler, acidic seal, sulphate-free + cool washing, re-tone/gloss at 4–6 weeks — chemistry matched to diagnosis, not force.' },
