      deepTheory: `<p>Consultation is risk management disguised as conversation. Industry complaint data consistently traces most disputes to three failures: unrecorded history, untested assumptions (especially previous colour/chemicals), and expectations never calibrated to what the hair can actually deliver. Your consultation therefore has three outputs, every time: a <b>diagnosis</b> (what the hair/scalp is), a <b>contract</b> (what you will do, cost, time, maintenance), and a <b>record</b> (signed card that protects both parties).</p><p><b>The 7-point framework, expanded:</b> (1) Lifestyle audit — styling time budget, heat habits, wash frequency, occupation (hard hats, food hygiene, swimming), upcoming events. A 45-minute blow-dry routine will not survive a client who air-dries. (2) Chemical history — every tint, bleach, perm, relaxer, keratin and box dye for 2+ years, including henna/metallic salts that react violently with lightener. Ask twice; clients forget box black. (3) Medical flags — pregnancy, breastfeeding, chemotherapy, scalp conditions, allergies (PPD, latex, fragrance), medications causing shedding or sensitivity. (4) Scalp + hair examination — 4 quadrants under good light, the full quartet from Week 2, elasticity non-negotiable before chemicals. (5) Face-shape, growth patterns and lifestyle match — show two options with mirror and reference photos, check cowlicks, partings and glasses. (6) Honest plan — state what is achievable in how many visits; show the price and time before touching the hair; get explicit verbal consent plus signature for chemical services. (7) Aftercare + rebooking — prescribe home-care, state the maintenance interval, pre-book.</p><p><b>Contraindication triage (RAG):</b> RED — refuse and refer: pediculosis, tinea, impetigo, undiagnosed lumps, open wounds, severe inflamed scalp, known allergy to planned product. AMBER — adapt with consent: mild dandruff (gentle approach), controlled psoriasis plaques (avoid, work around, refer if spreading), pregnancy (avoid strong chemicals/fumes, postpone major colour changes, ventilate), recent keratin (limits lightening). GREEN — proceed with standard protocol. Document the RAG rating on every card.</p><p><b>Communication mechanics that close without pressure:</b> open questions first ('walk me through your routine'), reflective listening ('so the brassiness bothers you most at week three'), visual aids (swatches, wheel, before/afters of similar hair — never celebrity photos on incompatible textures), the repeat-back ('so we agree: global gloss today, highlights next visit, £X, 2 hours, purple shampoo at home — correct?'), and the honest no ('your hair cannot survive platinum today; here is the staged plan'). Silence after quoting is a tool — let the client decide.</p><p><b>Records and law:</b> keep consultation cards 3+ years (check local requirements), including analysis, formula, tests, consent signature and aftercare given. Photos need explicit consent stating salon/portfolio use. Never diagnose medical conditions — your vocabulary is 'I can see X, which needs a pharmacist/GP/dermatologist; here is what I can safely do meanwhile.' Data protection: cards contain health-adjacent personal data; store securely, never photograph cards onto personal phones.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — allergy and sensitivity screening script:</b> 'Have you ever reacted to hair colour, black henna tattoos, rubber gloves, fragrances or plasters? Are you pregnant, breastfeeding, or undergoing medical treatment? Any scalp itching, flaking or sores today?' Any yes to reactions = mandatory 48h skin test and heightened watch; PPD allergy confirmed = no oxidative tint ever (offer semi/demi deposit-only or highlift-free alternatives where safe, documented). Latex allergy = nitrile gloves stocked. Keep antihistamine-free protocol: your job is prevention and referral, never treatment.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The Pinterest platinum', brief: 'A new client shows a photo of icy platinum on waist-length hair. Her history: black box dye 6 weeks ago, plus a keratin smoothing treatment 2 months ago. She has 2 hours and wants it today. Conduct the consultation to standard.', tasks: ['Identify every red/amber flag in history + examination and state what each rules out.', 'Write your staged alternative plan (visits, services, tests) with honest timelines.', 'Script the exact words you use to decline today while keeping her as a client.', 'List what goes on the card even though no service is performed.'], debrief: 'Box black + keratin + 2-hour window = no-go for platinum today (incompatibility risk, insufficient lift time, integrity unknown). Staged plan: strand tests now, clarifying + trim + gloss today, correction stages booked out. Script centres honesty + alternative + rebook. Card records history, flags, tests offered, advice given, signature.' },
