      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Precision cutting is applied geometry. Four variables control every result — <b>section</b> (where hair is isolated), <b>elevation</b> (degrees lifted from the head: 0° natural fall, 45° graduation, 90° uniform layering, 180° long layers), <b>over-direction</b> (combing away from natural fall to move weight), and <b>cutting line</b> (blunt, point, slide, chip). Change one variable deliberately; if a result surprises you, one of the four drifted.</p><p><b>Sectioning architecture:</b> the classic 4-quarter (centre profile + ear-to-ear) plus horseshoe (crown-to-recession) gives 7 controllable zones; add pivoting radial sections for round layering. Subsections ≤1 cm thick and ≤ the shear blade length — thicker sections force the bottom to travel further than the top, which is the hidden cause of most uneven perimeters. Partings must be clean to the scalp; every stray hair in the wrong section becomes a hole in the line. Clip order: work nape → sides → front so cut hair never contaminates uncut zones.</p><p><b>Tension physics:</b> even, moderate tension projects every subsection to the same spatial point — uneven tension is the #1 cause of ledges. Stretch, never pull the scalp; fine hair needs lighter tension (it stretches more, so heavy tension over-cuts); curly/coily hair must be cut with minimal tension or in its natural state, because each curl type shrinks differently when dry (shrinkage 20–75% by curl pattern — the reason wet-cutting curls uniform lengths produces staggered dry results). Comb flat to the subsection, fingers parallel to the cutting line, body positioned so the line is sighted straight — cutting across your own eyeline guarantees a travelling line.</p><p><b>The one-length (solid form) protocol:</b> 0° elevation throughout, stationary guide (each new subsection measured against the previously cut one, 1–2 cm overlap), cut palm-to-palm with the blades' mid-section (tips wander), head upright then chin-to-chest to expose the nape gradient. Cross-check in three states — wet combed left, wet combed right, and dry with natural fall — plus the client upright and looking at you (faces are asymmetric; the line must read level on the person, not the floor).</p><p><b>Shear mechanics for clean cutting:</b> sharp convex blades slice; blunt or loose shears push and fold hair, causing split-prone crushed ends. Cut with the mid-blade, close decisively (hesitant micro-cuts chew the line), never cut past the second knuckle near skin, call 'closing' at ears. Point-cutting (tips into the ends, 1–2 cm) softens perimeters for lived-in bobs; slide-cutting (gliding partially-open blades) removes bulk along lengths on straight, healthy hair only — never on fragile or curly hair where it shreds.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — shear maintenance log:</b> daily: wipe blades, close and store covered; weekly: 1 drop of shear oil at the pivot, worked through, wiped clean; monthly: drop-test tension, check for nicks under light, tighten/loosen pivot a quarter-turn at a time; yearly (or 500–700 cuts): professional sharpening — never grinding wheels, which destroy temper. Dropped shears go straight to the sharpener for inspection: a chipped convex edge crushes every cut until re-ground. Log it like colour formulas; edges are inventory.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The travelling bob', brief: 'Your mannequin one-length photographs level from behind, but from the front the left side hangs 1.5 cm longer. Diagnose the geometric fault and correct it.', tasks: ['Name the three most likely causes (tension, body position, guide) and how to test each.', 'Explain why cutting from one side of the mannequin guarantees this result.', 'Write the corrected cutting order with cross-checks that would have caught it.', 'How does this fault behave differently on curly hair, and what changes?'], debrief: 'Classic causes: drifting tension, sighting across the eyeline from a fixed stance, losing the stationary guide. Cure: move your body to face each section, re-establish guide every 2 subsections, triple cross-check. On curls the same fault hides wet and ambushes dry — cut with minimal tension and verify dry.' },
