      activity: { type: 'cards', title: 'Face-Shape Prescriptions', instructions: 'Match shape to prescription.', items: [['Round', 'Height + length, flat sides'], ['Square', 'Soften jaw, side part'], ['Heart', 'Weight at chin/jaw'], ['Long', 'Width + fringe'], ['Oval', 'Most styles work']] },
      threeD: { type: 'hair', title: '3D Lab: Weight Maps', description: 'Heat-map view: graduation stacks weight low, layers distribute it. Rotate to see silhouette change.' },
      quiz: [
        { q: 'Graduated bob elevation ~', options: ['45°', '0°', '180°', '270°'], answer: 0, explain: 'Creates stacked weight.' },
        { q: 'Round face needs:', options: ['Height + length', 'Wide volume', 'Bowl fringe', 'Buzz'], answer: 0, explain: 'Elongate, narrow sides.' },
        { q: 'Layers mainly:', options: ['Remove weight + add movement', 'Add weight at perimeter', 'Straighten permanently', 'Add colour'], answer: 0, explain: 'Projection distributes weight.' },
        { q: 'Wet vs dry length:', options: ['Wet looks longer — cut cautiously', 'Identical', 'Dry longer', 'Ignore'], answer: 0, explain: 'Hair shrinks up when dry, esp. curly.' },
        { q: 'Blending tool:', options: ['Point/slide cutting', 'Razor on wet skin', 'Thinning everything', 'Fire'], answer: 0, explain: 'Softens lines without holes.' }
      ],
      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Graduation and layering are weight-management systems. <b>Graduation (1–89° elevation, classically ~45°)</b> builds stacked weight: shorter interior lengths push longer exterior ones outward, creating the bevelled bob silhouette. The stack angle is controlled by finger angle, not just elevation — fingers angled under build weight low (classic bob), fingers vertical build a uniform stack. Over-direction forward through the front preserves length at the face while stacking the back; over-direction backward does the reverse. <b>Layering (90°+ uniform, 180° long layers)</b> distributes weight evenly through projection: 90° gives even, rounded volume; 180° (over-directed to the top/forward) preserves perimeter length while removing interior bulk — the long-layer and 'butterfly' logic.</p><p><b>Weight diagnostics:</b> ledges (horizontal shelves) = elevation dropped mid-section or guide lost — re-project and re-cut, never notch the ledge out; holes = over-point-cutting or thinning shear abuse — grow out, camouflage with styling, document; corners (heavy front edges on bobs) = under-over-direction — re-cut with forward over-direction; triangle (wide bottom, flat top on curly hair) = layers too long for the pattern — shorten interior layers and widen the silhouette deliberately.</p><p><b>Face-shape science beyond the basics:</b> measure thirds (hairline–brow, brow–nose base, nose–chin) and fifths (eye widths across) to classify objectively: oval (length 1.5× width — the balancer everything is compared to), round (length ≈ width, soft angles — needs vertical emphasis: height at crown, length past chin, flat sides), square (strong jaw ≈ forehead — needs softening: side parts, texture, length past the jaw, no blunt jaw-length lines), heart (wide forehead, narrow chin — needs chin weight: bobs ending at jaw, curtain fringe, no crown height), oblong/long (needs width: fringe to shorten, horizontal volume, no extra crown height), diamond (narrow forehead + chin, wide cheeks — needs forehead and chin coverage). Glasses, hijab/ headwear, and collar lines modify every prescription — consult the whole head, not the face alone.</p><p><b>Blending arsenal, parameterised:</b> point-cutting 1–3 cm into ends at varied depths softens without visible steps; slide-cutting removes interior bulk on straight healthy hair (blade angle ~30°, slow glide, never on fragile/curly); thinning shears (20–40 teeth) remove 10–30% per close — maximum 2 closes per section, mid-lengths only, never at roots (regrowth spikes) or perimeters (wispy edges); razor cutting (guarded, wet hair, 45° glide) creates shattered lived-in texture but lifts cuticle — reserve for coarse straight hair, never fine/high-porosity. Dry-check every graduation and layer: wet hair lies 1–2 cm longer and hides every sin.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — thinning-shear discipline:</b> 20–40 teeth remove 10–30% per close; maximum 2 closes per section, mid-lengths only, on dry or damp (never wet-fragile) straight-to-wavy healthy hair. Forbidden zones: roots (spikes), perimeters (wispy collapse), curly/coily hair (frizz explosion + holes), fragile/bleached lengths (shredding). Document every thinning-shear use on the card — the next stylist inherits your removals for 6+ months of grow-out.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The triangular curls', brief: 'A client with dense 3b curls arrives with a pyramid: flat crown, massive wide ends. She wants shape without losing length. Plan the correction.', tasks: ['Explain the structural cause (layer length vs curl pattern) in weight terms.', 'Specify the layering system: elevation, sectioning pattern, tension rules for curls.', 'Which blending tools are forbidden here, and why?', 'Write the styling + home-care that sells and preserves the shape (diffusing, products, trim cycle).'], debrief: 'Long uniform lengths collapse at the crown and bulk at the ends on tight patterns. Fix: rounded 90°+ layers with minimal tension, dry or stretched verification, no thinning shears/slide (frizz + holes on curls). Sell with diffused volume demo, curl cream + gel cast routine, 8–12 week reshape cycle.' },
