      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Heat styling is controlled hydrogen-bond engineering. Hair's shape memory lives in hydrogen bonds (water/heat-reversible) while disulphide bonds hold the permanent pattern — blow-drying and ironing rearrange the former without touching the latter, which is why humidity reverses your work and why heat damage is cumulative, not self-repairing. Damage markers to teach every client: bubble hair (steam blisters inside the shaft from ironing damp hair — visible under magnification), white-dot breakage nodes, loss of elasticity, and ends that lighten and frizz (cuticle stripped, cortex exposed).</p><p><b>Dryer physics and modern hardware:</b> airflow (measured in m³/min and velocity) does the drying; heat accelerates it but raises damage exponentially above ~200°C on damp hair. Ionic generators emit negative ions that break water into smaller droplets (faster evaporation, less frizz); far-infrared heat penetrates more evenly than element heat; intelligent dryers (scalp-sensing infrared thermometers, e.g. AirLight-style) auto-reduce heat near the scalp. Protocol: 80% rough-dry on medium heat with fingers or paddle (removes bulk water fast, minimal tension damage), then sectioned smoothing — nozzle with concentrator, airflow always down-shaft, brush tension firm but not scalp-dragging, each section dried 100% (damp-cool spots collapse within the hour), cool shot to set.</p><p><b>Brush grammar:</b> paddle for sleek mid-lengths; round brush diameter ≈ curl size wanted (small = curl/tension on short hair and fringes, large = volume and sweep on long hair); ceramic/thermal barrels hold heat for faster sets but burn inexperienced hands — keep moving. Root lift = over-direction + heat at the base, then cool in position; a Velcro roller pinned at the crown while you finish buys volume that survives the commute. Setting (rollers, pin curls) works on the same bond physics with zero heat: damp-set, dry 100% under ambient or hood heat, cool fully before removing — impatience is the only failure mode.</p><p><b>Thermal iron parameters:</b> fine/damaged/chemically treated ≤185°C; medium healthy 185–200°C; coarse resistant ≤210–230°C — always starting low and doing a test pass. One slow, steady pass (3–5 seconds per section) beats multiple fast scrubs; clamp pressure light (dent lines come from clamping, not heat); heat protectant mandatory (film-formers + silicones that buffer to ~230°C and add slip); never iron damp hair (bubble hair), never same-day as fresh bleach without bond care. Curling with irons: bevelled plates and rounded housings prevent crease lines; wrapping (hair around the tool) gives soft waves, clamping (tool through hair) gives defined curls.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — heat damage triage ladder:</b> stage 1 (dry, slight fade): trim dusting + mask rotation + lower heat 10–15°C; stage 2 (white dots, elasticity dropping): cut above the worst nodes, bond treatment course, heat pause 2–4 weeks; stage 3 (gummy, smoking history, breakage): significant cut, protein/moisture rehabilitation plan, no chemicals until elasticity recovers, documented re-tests. Never promise to 'repair' split ends with product — split ends are cut off; products only camouflage and protect what remains.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The collapsing blow-dry', brief: 'Your volume blow-dry looks red-carpet in the chair but falls flat by the time the client reaches her car (20 minutes). Audit the failure chain.', tasks: ['Rank the likely culprits: incomplete drying, no cool-set, product overload, wrong brush size — with a test for each.', 'Rewrite the protocol for fine, low-density hair specifically (products, sections, heat, set).', 'What do you change for coarse, high-density hair in the same 30-minute slot?', 'Write the 5-minute home version you teach her, with the one product she must own.'], debrief: 'Collapse = damp-cool spots + no heat/cool setting + heavy product. Fine hair: light mousse at roots only, small sections, over-directed heat, cool pinned, light spray. Coarse hair: richer protectant, larger sections, higher (safe) heat, longer cool. Home version: rough-dry + crown roller + cool shot — one styler, five minutes.' },
