      deepTheory: `<p>Occasion hair is structural engineering under time pressure: the style must survive hugs, wind, dancing and photography flash for 8+ hours. The load path runs <b>prep → anchor → shape → dress</b>, and failures almost always trace to skipped prep or a misplaced anchor. <b>Prep chemistry:</b> clean hair has low friction (slippery) — build grip with mousse at roots (dried in for memory), texture/dry-shampoo spray through lengths (grit), and light backcombing at the anchor zone (a cushion of interlocked hair that pins bite into). Working sprays (brushable hold) during construction, finishing spray (firm hold) only at the end — reversing the order glues sections before they are placed.</p><p><b>Braid mechanics:</b> 3-strand over-braid (flat, classic), French (feeding sides inward — tension must stay constant or the braid wanders), Dutch/under-braid (pops 3D, the volume choice), fishtail (two bundles, fine feeds — slowest but most intricate), rope/twist (two twisted strands coiled — fastest elegant option), waterfall and halo variations for bridal. Feed size controls definition: fine feeds = intricate and slow, chunky feeds = soft and fast. Pancaking (gently pulling braid edges) widens up to 30% — pancake as you go, because dried spray locks the width.</p><p><b>Anchor physics:</b> weight must centre over a skull shelf — occipital bone for chignons, crown padding for height styles, nape for low knots. Padding (donut, mesh cushion matched to hair colour) creates volume the client's density cannot supply. Pin science: insert ripple-side down (waves grip), against the direction of pull, two pins crossed per load point, ends tucked so no metal shows or scratches. U-pins for soft holds, bun pins for structure, spin pins (screw-in) for heavy buns — three spin pins replace a dozen bobby pins. Pain test: if the client feels pulling now, it will be agony by hour four — redistribute immediately.</p><p><b>Bridal system:</b> trial 4–8 weeks out with the veil/accessories present, white and black backdrop photos in 3 angles plus a video turn, wear-test minimum 2 hours, written formula (pins count, products, time taken — the wedding day runs to your trial time plus 15 minutes buffer). Contract essentials: arrival time, location, party size, price per head, overtime rate, touch-up kit contents, cancellation terms. Emergency kit: pins, mini spray, shine sheets, static guard, needle/thread, fashion tape, painkillers, water, phone charger — the kit is the difference between a crisis and a story.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — bridal timing template (per head):</b> trial 60–90 min including photos and notes; wedding day: bride 60–75 min, attendants 40–50 min each, mother-of-bride 40 min; buffer 15 min per 3 heads plus 30 min contingency before photography. Price structure: trial (redeemable on booking), per-head day rate, early-start fee before 7am, travel per km, overtime per 30 min — all contracted with party size, arrival time and cancellation terms. Touch-up kit per bride: pins, mini spray, shine sheets, fashion tape, painkillers, water.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The slipping chignon', brief: 'A bridesmaid with fine, freshly washed slippery hair needs a low chignon to last a 10-hour wedding day. Your trial version sagged within an hour. Re-engineer it.', tasks: ['Diagnose the prep failure (clean hair + no grip + wrong anchor?) with specifics.', 'Specify the corrected prep stack: products, backcombing zones, drying method.', 'Choose the anchor + padding + pin system with counts, and justify each.', 'Write the wedding-day timing plan and the contents of her touch-up kit.'], debrief: 'Slippery + unprepped + under-pinned = sag. Fix: dirty-hair simulation (dry shampoo + mousse dried in), occipital anchor with colour-matched cushion, crossed ripple pins (12–16) plus 2 spin pins, working spray during + firm finish after. Trial time + 15 min buffer on the day; kit with pins, mini spray, tape and charger.' },
