      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Colour theory is physics (light and pigment) driving chemistry (oxidation). Natural hair colour comes from melanin density and type in the cortex: <b>eumelanin</b> (brown/black, dense oval granules — hard to lighten past orange) and <b>pheomelanin</b> (red/yellow, diffuse — exposes fast and warm). The <b>level system 1–10</b> measures depth (1 black → 10 lightest blonde); <b>tone</b> (.0 natural, .1 ash/blue, .2 violet/pearl, .3 gold, .4 copper, .5 mahogany, .6 red, .7 bronze/green-brown) describes the reflect on top. Underlying pigment ('contributing pigment') is exposed stage by stage as you lift: 1–4 red → 5–6 orange → 7–8 yellow-orange → 9–10 pale yellow — and every formulation must account for what the lift will reveal, not just what the tube promises.</p><p><b>Oxidation mechanics:</b> permanent tint couples dye precursors ( incl. PPD/PTD in dark shades — the lifelong-allergy molecule) with couplers inside the cortex, driven by hydrogen peroxide developer. Developer strength sets lift: 10 vol (3%) deposits/darkens with minimal lift; 20 vol (6%) lifts 1–2 + standard grey coverage; 30 vol (9%) lifts 2–3; 40 vol (12%) maximum lift with maximum damage — pros use it rarely and never on compromised hair. Mix ratios (1:1, 1:1.5, 1:2 by brand) control alkalinity and processing: richer developer = more lift, more swelling, more damage. Weigh on digital gram scales — 'glugs' are how corrections are born.</p><p><b>Colour categories, precisely:</b> temporary (coated pigments, 1 wash — event colour, no commitment); semi-permanent (direct dyes that stain, 4–8 washes, no developer, no lift — gloss refreshes and fashion tones on pre-lightened hair); demi-permanent (low-volume oxidative, deposits and blends grey up to ~50%, minimal lift — the retouch-line and ends-refresh workhorse); permanent (full oxidative lift + deposit, 100% grey coverage with N series, needs regrowth maintenance); lighteners/bleach (persulphate + alkaline lift with no tone deposited — must always be followed by toner/gloss). Grey hair lacks melanin and often has a resistant cuticle: N (natural) base supplies the missing background pigment — fashion-only shades slide off grey.</p><p><b>Testing law:</b> skin (allergy alert) test 48 hours before every oxidative service on new clients, after reactions, or after gaps in service — dab mixed tint behind the ear/inner elbow, no rinsing games, record the result. Strand test predicts lift, tone, timing and condition on the actual head: apply the exact formula to a discreet section, process fully, read level + elasticity + porosity change. Contraindications that cancel colour: active allergy signs, broken/inflamed scalp, recent severe reaction, incompatible history (metallic/compound henna + lightener = smoking green melt — cut it out, do not bleach over it).</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — developer chart (memorise):</b> 10 vol / 3% = deposit, darken, gloss, minimal lift; 20 vol / 6% = 1–2 levels lift + standard grey coverage; 30 vol / 9% = 2–3 levels on healthy hair; 40 vol / 12% = maximum lift, maximum damage, foundation-level forbidden without supervision. Higher volume also means faster swelling, more cuticle disruption and warmer exposed underlying pigment — which is why grey coverage and even results usually want 20 vol discipline, not 30 vol force. On-scalp lightener rules (where permitted): 20–30 vol max, barrier protection, never overlapping previously lightened hair.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The box-black blonde', brief: 'A client with 6 months of black box dye wants honey blonde balayage today, "just a few pieces to brighten". Her elasticity is borderline. Formulate the professional response.', tasks: ['Explain what box black + borderline elasticity means for lift (stages, banding, breakage risk).', 'Design the staged plan: today, visit 2, visit 3 — services, formulas, tests, prices.', 'Write the strand-test protocol you run before promising anything.', 'Script declining "today" while booking the correction course.'], debrief: 'Box dye lifts unpredictably with bands; borderline elasticity forbids aggressive lifting. Today: strand tests + gloss/trim + plan, never full balayage. Stages: gentle test panels → progressive foiling with bond care → tone. Price the course, photograph stages, consent each step — corrections are sold as journeys.' },
