      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Full colour and retouch work is where formulation meets logistics — saturation, timing and overlap control across a whole head. <b>Virgin application:</b> on previously uncoloured hair, apply to mid-lengths and ends first (they process slowest, away from scalp heat), then roots last (scalp heat accelerates ~1 level) — or follow brand-specific direction, which overrides habit. <b>Retouch:</b> regrowth only, ~1 cm subsections, saturate without flooding the scalp; overlapping onto previously coloured hair creates bands (darker, duller stripes) that compound every visit. Ends refresh: demi-permanent or gloss pulled through for the final 5–10 minutes only.</p><p><b>Grey-coverage formulation, worked example:</b> 70% resistant grey wanting level-6 copper. Formula: ½ 6N + ½ 6.4 (or 6.46) with 20 vol, 1:1, full 35–45 minutes, fine 0.5 cm sections for saturation. The N supplies background pigment grey lacks; the .4 supplies the fashion reflect; 20 vol opens the resistant cuticle without unnecessary lift; fine sections prevent translucent coverage. Resistant temples/hairline get first application (longest processing). White (>90%) hair wanting fashion red needs the same N ballast or it glows translucent pink within two washes.</p><p><b>Processing control:</b> start the timer at first contact and note the finish order — first-applied processes longest, so plan the sequence (resistant areas first, porous ends last). Check at three-quarter time: wipe one subsection to read undertone and coverage. Emulsify the final 5 minutes (add water, massage — evens tone and eases rinsing). Cool-to-lukewarm rinse (hot water swells and bleeds fresh colour), colour-safe sulphate-free shampoo, acidic conditioner to reseal. Scalp staining is removed with stain remover or barrier cream next time — prevention (barrier cream on hairline/ears, no scalp flooding) beats cure.</p><p><b>Banding and correction basics:</b> bands come from overlap, under-saturation, or timing drift. Minor banding: re-apply carefully to the band only, feathering 5 mm either side, process visually, gloss to blend. Severe multi-band or box-dye histories graduate to the Chemical course's corrective protocols — recognise the boundary and refer upward rather than improvising with stronger developer. Every service ends with the formula card: brand + shade + vol + ratio + grams + processing time + result photo. No card, no rebook — the next stylist (possibly you, in 6 weeks) is flying blind.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — grey-coverage ladder:</b> up to 30% grey: fashion shade + 10–20 vol often suffices; 30–70%: add N (½ N + ½ fashion) with 20 vol; 70–100% resistant: N-dominant (¾ N + ¼ fashion) or N alone at target level, 20 vol, fine sections, full time, resistant hairline first. White non-pigmented hair grabs fashion tone translucently — always ballast with N. Coarse resistant grey may need dedicated resistant-grey series or pre-softening per brand; document what worked, because grey patterns are stable and the formula repeats for years.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The banded brunette', brief: 'A client arrives with visible horizontal bands: dark regrowth, a lighter orange band mid-lengths, darker ends. History: home root touch-ups overlapping for a year. She wants "one even chocolate brown today". Plan it.', tasks: ['Map the three zones and state what each needs (lift? fill? deposit?) — and what must NOT be overlapped.', 'Write the formula per zone with volumes, ratios and processing order.', 'Explain why a single all-over tint today would fail.', 'Set the maintenance that prevents the next band (retouch discipline + ends refresh).'], debrief: 'Overlap history = bands. Single tint darkens everything unevenly and compounds the problem. Zone plan: roots with N+fashion 20 vol, band feathered with demi or careful re-application, ends glossed; process roots first, blend at the end. Maintenance: roots-only discipline, gloss refreshes, 4–6 week cycle with cards.' },
