      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Dimensional blonding is lightener control plus placement design. <b>Lightener mechanics:</b> persulphate + alkaline cream/gel + developer lifts by diffusing melanin; powder lighteners lift fastest (most alkaline, most swelling — off-scalp only unless formulated for scalp), cream lighteners offer control for on-scalp and balayage. Fresh mix per application (potency decays from the moment of mixing), 20 vol for controlled lift, 30 vol for higher lift on healthy hair, 40 vol almost never at foundation level. <b>Bond protection</b> (Olaplex-style No.1 in the lightener, K18 mist or equivalent after) is now standard care, not luxury — it preserves elasticity through the lift so hair survives to be toned.</p><p><b>Foil systems:</b> weave (fine alternating strands left out — soft, office-friendly dimension), slice (solid panels — bold ribbons and money-pieces), back-to-back vs skipped foils (density control), bricklay staggering (diffuses lines between rows). Saturation edge-to-edge with a feathered root (tap the brush, don't push to the scalp — lightener swell touches skin and burns). Seal firmly; air gaps stall processing and leak. Check every 10 minutes: wipe a foil edge, read the undercoat, pull-test elasticity. Never re-apply fresh lightener over already-lightened hair — overlap is instant breakage.</p><p><b>Balayage and lived-in methods:</b> hand-painted sweeps on a board, V and W motions, feathered 2–3 cm transition zones, open-air (slower, softer) or filmed (faster, brighter). Teasylights/foilyage hybrid: backcomb the section, paint the ends, foil for extra lift — the diffused grow-out clients pay premiums for. Foilage (foil + balayage placement), colour melting (smudged root-to-mid blend with demi), and money-piece framing complete the modern menu. Grow-out design is part of the formulation: feathered roots buy 8–12 weeks; harsh lines buy complaints at week 4.</p><p><b>Toning science:</b> toner deposits reflect on a pre-lightened canvas — it cannot lift, so ash on orange goes muddy, never clean. Rules: lift to pale yellow (9–10) for ash/pearl/violet; level 8 yellow takes gold/beige; damp hair for even uptake; 5–20 minutes visual checks every 5; cool rinse. Purple/blue shampoos maintain (1× weekly — daily use dulls and dries). Integrity abort criteria, non-negotiable: gummy stretch, smoking, scalp pain — rinse now, protein + moisture + trim plan, honest rebook. Document: natural level, formula, vol, timing, elasticity notes, toner + time, photo at each stage.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — toner selection matrix:</b> base pale yellow 9–10 + unwanted yellow → violet/pearl; + unwanted gold → blue-violet; level 8 yellow + want beige → gold-violet balance; orange remnants → blue (only after further lift — toner cannot fix level 7 orange into ash). Processing: damp hair, 5–20 min visual, check every 5, cool rinse, acidic seal. Over-toning signs: dull, grey cast, lavender grab on porous ends — remove with clarifying + warm gloss, adjust next formula weaker or shorter. Porous ends grab: pre-fill or dilute toner on the most porous zones.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The brassy balayage', brief: 'A client returns at week 3: balayage gone brassy orange-gold, ends dry, grow-out line visible at 2 cm. She wants "fixed and ashy" in one visit. Diagnose and plan.', tasks: ['Explain the three faults (under-lifted base, porous ends grabbing warmth, harsh blend zone) with the science of each.', 'Decide: re-lift, tone-only, or staged? Justify with elasticity and level logic.', 'Write the correction formula(s): lightener placement, toner choice, timing, bond care.', 'Set maintenance (purple rotation, gloss cycle, trim) that holds the ash to week 8.'], debrief: 'Brass = lift stopped at orange-gold + porous ends. If elasticity allows: feathered re-lift of warm zones with bond care, then blue-violet toner on a truly pale canvas; if gummy: tone what exists, treat, stage the lift. Maintenance: purple 1×/week, gloss at 4–6, dusting trims — ash is a maintenance colour, say so upfront.' },
