      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Salon-readiness is a business system, not just technique. <b>Retail science:</b> clients buy outcomes they can feel — diagnose aloud ('your ends are high-porosity, so they drink conditioner and fade fast'), prescribe maximum three items (shampoo + conditioner + one hero), demo each on their hair during the service, quantify dose ('a 10p coin, mid-lengths down'), and state the repurchase trigger ('this bottle is ~3 months at your wash rate'). Track close rates: 20–30% of serviced clients buying is a healthy foundation target; below 10% means diagnosis is not being voiced.</p><p><b>Rebooking mechanics:</b> offer two specific times before the cape comes off ('same time Thursday, or Saturday morning?'), state the scientifically correct interval (cuts 4–8 weeks by style, tint retouch 4–6, toner refresh 4–6, treatments 2–4), send a 48-hour reminder, and follow up non-rebookers once with value ('your gloss will need its refresh — shall I hold Thursday?'). Pre-booking rate above 60% is the stability line for a column; measure it weekly.</p><p><b>Complaint recovery (the service-recovery paradox — well-handled failures create more loyal clients than flawless service):</b> listen without interrupting, apologise for the experience not the person, examine under proper lighting, restate the issue to confirm, offer a concrete fix with a fair window (cuts 7 days, colour 14 days), perform the correction graciously, log root cause, and follow up. Never argue chemistry with an upset client in the chair — fix first, analyse later. Log every complaint: patterns (same banding fault twice) are system failures, not bad luck.</p><p><b>Portfolio and personal brand:</b> before/after in identical light, angle and distance, signed consent specifying portfolio/social use, honest captions (formulas build authority; filters destroy it), 10+ looks minimum covering cuts, styling and colour-assists. Reviews: ask at the glow moment with a QR code, respond to every review within 48 hours, never incentivise dishonestly. <b>Assessment standard:</b> 30-question theory at ≥70% plus an observed full service scored against the rubric below — the same bar every graduate clears, which is what makes the certificate mean something.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — column maths (know your numbers):</b> rebook rate = rebooked ÷ serviced (target &gt;60%); retail attach = buyers ÷ serviced (target 20–30%); average ticket = revenue ÷ clients (track monthly, grow via attach + upgrades not discounts); complaint rate = complaints ÷ 100 services (target &lt;2%, every one logged with cause); portfolio velocity = new documented looks per month (target ≥4). Review these five numbers on the 1st of each month — professionals manage columns by metrics, amateurs by hope.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The unhappy blonde', brief: 'A colour client messages 5 days later: "too dark and ashy, not what I asked for." She is polite but firm, and has posted a lukewarm review. Execute the recovery to standard.', tasks: ['Write your reply message (tone, acknowledgement, next step) — and what you must NOT write.', 'Plan the correction appointment: diagnosis possibilities (over-toned? under-lifted showing ash? lighting shock?) and fix options.', 'State the fix window policy and how you log this for root-cause analysis.', 'Turn the recovery into retention + review repair: what do you ask, and when?'], debrief: 'Reply within hours: acknowledge, invite back, no defensiveness in writing. Diagnose in chair (lighting shock vs over-tone vs level miss), correct within the 14-day colour window, log cause (consultation gap? timing? toner choice?). After she loves it: ask to update the review at the glow moment — recovered clients become evangelists.' },
